Looking at the EventBus implementation in `src/lib/eventBus.ts`:

```typescript
emit(event: string, payload: unknown) {
  for (const handler of handlers.get(event) ?? []) void Promise.resolve().then(() => handler(payload))
}
```

EventBus.emit treats its handlers as **fire-and-forget**. It wraps each handler in `Promise.resolve().then()` to schedule them asynchronously on the microtask queue, but never awaits them. The emit() call returns immediately without waiting for any handler to complete or seeing their errors.

This is why the test was flaky—the StockListener's async handler (which updates the database) could still be running when the test immediately checked the database after createOrder() returned.

